            'faqs' => [
                ['Will this teach me to start a business?', '<p>It teaches the thinking and the groundwork rather than handing over a template. Innovation for Business and Thinking Entrepreneurially address how opportunities are recognised and acted on, and your two chosen subjects let you go deeper into planning, marketing or the money side. What it cannot do is replace actually trading. Treat it as structure and credibility around what you are doing, not as a substitute for doing it.</p>', 1],
                ['Which two optional subjects should I pick?', '<p>It depends on where you are weakest rather than where you are strongest. If you have an idea but no route to market, entrepreneurial marketing earns its place. If you cannot yet answer how the venture makes money, take the finance and accounting subject. Business planning suits anyone who needs to put a proposal in front of a lender, an investor or a licensing authority. Your centre can advise, and should tell you which it actually runs.</p>', 2],
                ['Why is English a compulsory subject?', '<p>Because communication is most of the job. Qualifi makes English for Business Communication mandatory alongside the two enterprise subjects, which is a reasonable reflection of how founders spend their time: writing proposals, explaining the idea, negotiating terms and dealing with officialdom. It is the only Level 3 business award in Qualifi\'s range to require it.</p>', 3],
                ['Do I need business qualifications to enrol?', '<p>No. Three starting points are accepted: holding something at Level 2, having worked in the business or enterprise sector and being able to set out where you want to take that, or already holding a Level 3 award in an unrelated subject and wanting to switch into business. An existing venture or trading activity is relevant evidence. Admission runs through the centre, which makes the judgement.</p>', 4],
                ['Is it recognised outside the UK?', '<p>It is a UK qualification regulated by Ofqual and listed on the Register of Regulated Qualifications, and Qualifi states availability as UK and international. Acceptance by any particular employer, bank, university or licensing body remains a judgement those organisations make for themselves, and no awarding organisation can commit them in advance. If you need recognition for a specific purpose, verify it with that organisation first.</p>', 5],
