            ],
            'faqs' => [
                ['Does this let me work as a carer or a nurse?', '<p>No, and this is the single most important thing to understand before enrolling. Qualifi publishes nothing about registration with any health or care regulator for this award. It is a knowledge qualification that evidences what you understand about care practice. Permission to work in a regulated role is granted by the relevant authority in the country where you are, under its own rules, and no diploma from an awarding organisation substitutes for that. Check with the regulator directly.</p>', 1],
                ['Why are the optional subjects tied to specific conditions?', '<p>Because care work usually concentrates somewhere. Someone working in a dementia unit, a stroke rehabilitation setting or with people managing diabetes benefits more from depth in that area than from another general subject. Qualifi offering understanding of diabetes care, stroke care and dementia care as distinct choices is a practical piece of design and one of the clearer reasons to choose this award over a generic one.</p>', 2],
                ['How many optional subjects do I take?', '<p>The qualification page lists three as optional without stating how many are required, which is a gap in Qualifi\'s published information rather than something we are leaving out. The Centre Specification will answer it, and your centre can confirm before you commit. We would rather flag the gap than invent a number.</p>', 3],
                ['What does 600 hours mean in practice?', '<p>Qualifi uses total qualification time to estimate the whole effort a typical learner puts in, taught sessions and independent work alike, with preparation and assessment folded in. No part of it is a timetable, and none of it implies 600 hours sat in a classroom. It is useful as a measure of scale. For comparison, Qualifi\'s integrated Level 3 business award is published at 1,200 hours, so this sits at half that size.</p>', 4],
                ['Can I get in without any qualifications?', '<p>Possibly. Three doors are published. You may hold a Level 2 award; you may have worked in care and be able to say where you intend to take that; or you may already hold a Level 3 certificate in an unrelated field and now want to cross into this one. Practical sector experience is explicitly accepted, which suits people who have been doing the work without paperwork. The centre makes the decision.</p>', 5],
                ['Where can it take me afterwards?', '<p>Qualifi points to a Level 4 certificate, or to year one of a UK honours degree lasting three years. Universities decide their own admissions, so treat the degree route as a published possibility rather than an entitlement, and ask your target institutions what they accept. Within work, the qualification evidences knowledge; whether it leads to a promotion depends on your employer.</p>', 6],
            ],
            'meta_title' => 'Level 3 Diploma in Health and Social Care | Qualifi',
            'meta_description' => 'Qualifi Level 3 Diploma in Health and Social Care. 60 credits, 600 hours, options in diabetes, stroke and dementia care. Ofqual regulated. QAN 603/0819/9.',
        ],
