            'faqs' => [
                ['Can I become a solicitor with this?', '<p>Not with this alone, and not directly. Becoming a solicitor in England and Wales runs through the Solicitors Regulation Authority\'s own route, which has its own requirements for qualifying work experience and assessment. Qualifi publishes no SRA recognition for this award and does not claim it makes anyone eligible for anything. Treat it as legal knowledge you can build on, and take the route question to the SRA.</p>', 1],
                ['But Qualifi mentions the Solicitors Qualifying Examination.**
<p>It does, and the exact wording matters. Qualifi says its law suite "will aim to provide the underpinning knowledge of the law contributing to preparation for" that examination. Read it closely: aiming to provide knowledge that contributes to preparation. That is three layers of hedging, and it is a statement about subject knowledge, not about eligibility, exemption or recognition. We are not going to firm it up into something it is not.</p>

**Why is EU law still taught?', '<p>Qualifi lists it as one of the eight subjects and gives no explanation on the qualification page, so we will not invent one. A reasonable observation is that EU law remains relevant to anyone dealing with European counterparties, and that understanding why the Union was created and how its legal order works has value beyond membership. But that is our observation, not Qualifi\'s reasoning.</p>', 2],
                ['Eight subjects for 240 credits — is something missing?', '<p>Possibly. Most Qualifi awards at this credit size publish around twelve. The page gives no credit breakdown and says nothing about whether further content exists. Since Qualifi\'s own specification document covers the Level 4 and Level 5 law diplomas together, the published list may represent only part of the whole. Ask your centre for the complete specification before committing.</p>', 3],
                ['Do I need any qualifications to start?', '<p>None are published. Qualifi states only that applicants must be 18 or over, that IELTS 6 or equivalent applies where English is not your first language, and that two references are normally required with one preferably academic. The approved centre then judges whether you can meet the demands of the programme. Law reading is dense, so expect that judgement to be taken seriously.</p>', 4],
                ['Is this useful if I work outside the UK?', '<p>It can be, with clear eyes about what it is. The subjects are English law, so they will not tell you the rules where you live unless you live under that system. What transfers is the method: how obligations arise, how liability is analysed, how to read a statute and a case. In jurisdictions and financial free zones built on common law, that grounding has direct practical value.</p>', 5],
            ],
