            'faqs' => [
                ['Can I teach in a school with this?', '<p>No. Qualified Teacher Status is awarded through a separate route with its own training and assessment, and Qualifi publishes nothing about QTS, teacher registration or any licence to teach. Its own description points at teachers and trainers in the further education and training skills sector. If schools are your goal, this is the wrong qualification and you should look at initial teacher training instead.</p>', 1],
                ['Who is it genuinely useful for, then?', '<p>People who teach adults and young adults outside the school system. Corporate trainers, workplace assessors, college tutors, technical instructors, apprenticeship trainers, anyone running professional development in an organisation. It is particularly well suited to someone who already knows their subject thoroughly and has been handed teaching responsibility without ever being shown how learning is planned, delivered and assessed.</p>', 2],
                ['What happens if I do not finish the whole thing?', '<p>The provision on Qualifi\'s page reads that anyone falling short of the full extended award may be granted the Level 4 qualification, provided every Level 4 unit has been finished. Note the word may — it is weaker than a promise, and we are not going to strengthen it. If this provision matters to your decision, raise it with your centre before enrolling and ask them to confirm exactly how it is applied.</p>', 3],
                ['Do I need a Level 4 qualification first?', '<p>Qualifi envisages that learners entering at Level 5 will hold one, which is softer than requiring it. Alongside that, you need to be at least eighteen, show English at IELTS 6 or an equivalent Qualifi recognises, assuming English is not your mother tongue, and line up two referees, ideally including one from education. The centre also has to satisfy itself that you can handle the programme, so experience can form part of the conversation.</p>', 4],
                ['Two subjects look almost identical. Is that a mistake?', '<p>Professional Development and Reflective Practice appears at one stage and Reflective Practice and Professional Development at the other, which is the same two phrases reversed. Reading the award as the Level 4 diploma combined with Level 5 units, these are almost certainly the same theme revisited at greater depth. Qualifi does not state that explicitly, so ask your centre how the two differ in practice.</p>', 5],
                ['Does it cover managing a department, or only teaching?', '<p>Both, towards the end. Resource management in education, developing and evaluating operational work plans, and principles of educational leadership and management all sit in the later part of the programme. That makes it useful for someone moving from delivering training to running the function that delivers it. The management content is an addition to the teaching content, not a replacement for it.</p>', 6],
            ],
            'meta_title' => 'Level 5 Diploma in Education & Training Management | Qualifi',
            'meta_description' => 'Qualifi Level 5 Extended Diploma in Education and Training Management. 240 credits, 12 subjects for FE teachers and trainers. QAN 610/3648/1.',
        ],
